WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.080
- What's going on?

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.090
Welcome, welcome, welcome in

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:06.240
to another edition of "Market Takes".

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.830
I know you could have
been anywhere in the world

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.960
on this beautiful Fed
Day out here in New York,

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.120
but you're here with me
and I appreciate that.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.150
I'm Dion Rabouin, reporter
at "The Wall Street Journal".

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.630
We are breaking down
today's FOMC decision,

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.897
the Fed's decision, their
policy decision on rates,

00:00:21.897 --> 00:00:24.720
the Fed choosing to raise
rates by 25 basis points.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.250
This was really locked into markets,

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.287
but we did still see some market reaction

00:00:28.287 --> 00:00:29.880
and we're gonna talk through all that.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.590
Of course, we're gonna talk
about what the Fed did,

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.650
how that aligned with expectations
and the market reaction.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.930
We're gonna talk a little
bit about the environment

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.860
coming into this report and
really how what the Fed did

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.780
moved markets and how
it could move markets

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.100
going forward into the future.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.050
We're also gonna talk about
the Powell press conference

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.270
and what we learned from the Fed chairman,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.130
and of course talk about what comes next

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.720
because we are always looking forward

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.120
here on "Market Takes",
and that's what we do.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.740
So listen, before we go into
any more complicated analysis,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.870
let's just first talk
about what happened today.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.300
The Fed raised rates by 25 basis points.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.370
I'm gonna throw a chart
up here on your screen

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.990
so you can see just what we're looking at

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.000
in terms of action from the Fed.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.040
What you see there on
the right of your screen,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.970
the rate coming up really at
the fastest pace we have seen,

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.500
a lot of folks say in modern history,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.170
but really when you talk about
going from that zero level

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:23.250
to where we are now,
which is 5.25% to 5.5%,

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.440
that's really the fastest
rate when you think

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.420
about where we've come
from to where we are

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.620
that we have seen in history.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:33.210
So this rate hike cycle is kind
of unprecedented in history.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.920
And you see there just
kinda shoot straight up

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.480
for the first few months and then you get

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.090
that sort of slow down and
it looks more like an arc,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.910
more like what you had seen
before in those previous cycles.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:42.750
You'll also notice there

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.760
that those recession
shadings are in there.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.500
You see the 2001 recession,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.630
that 2008 global financial crisis

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.280
and the Great Recession as it's known,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.470
and then that 2020 COVID recession,

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.270
the shortest ever in history.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.090
Those all tend to coincide
with when rates are higher.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.110
That doesn't necessarily mean

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.180
that the high rates caused the recession

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.460
or that we necessarily have
to have a recession now,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.530
but do keep in mind, with rates,

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:05.880
as you see there on the
right of your screen,

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.730
the highest they have been since 2001,

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.710
that is definitely something
to keep an eye out for

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.470
because the economy struggles
with those higher rates.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.000
It's harder for businesses
to borrow money.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.490
It's more expensive, harder
for folks to borrow money

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.520
by borrowing against their
home with those home loans.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.260
And so it's harder for
people to get credit,

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.180
harder for businesses to grow,

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.310
harder to pay off some of
those debts and those things,

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.820
and so that tends to
lead to a slower economy

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.280
and a lot of times drives
the economy into recession.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.080
That's not what the Federal
Reserve wants to see.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.940
That's not what the data shows us,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.710
but it is something to
just keep your eye out for.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:41.070
And this chart really just
illustrating how far we've come

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.650
in terms of raising rates and
getting 'em to that high level

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.650
that you see there, 5.25
to 5.5% where we are today.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.210
Really, one of the biggest

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.760
and most interesting
things about the report

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.770
wasn't even what we
heard from the Chairman.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.020
That's usually the big blockbuster thing.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.050
It's what we got in the Fed
statement or the FOMC statement

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:04.320
from the Committee, which
had almost no changes.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.660
Usually when the Federal
Reserve makes a change,

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.400
like last month, they didn't raise rates,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.460
they held pat, didn't raise rates at all,

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.710
This month they did. They
raised 25 basis points.

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:16.170
So when you get a change
in policy like that,

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.760
usually there's a long explanation.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.707
There's, "We see this change
in the economy. We saw that.

00:03:20.707 --> 00:03:23.347
"This moved. Our analysis has changed.

00:03:23.347 --> 00:03:25.200
"Our view of the economy has changed."

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.750
This month's statement,
almost nothing changed

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.257
except for the fact the Fed said,

00:03:29.257 --> 00:03:31.657
"Yeah, we raised interest
rates to five and a quarter

00:03:31.657 --> 00:03:32.760
"to five and a half,"

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.590
which is how they'll put
it in their statement.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.710
And they said the economy
expand or economic activity

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:42.000
has expanded a, quote, "Moderate
rather than a modest pace."

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.490
Other than that, no real
changes to the statement

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:45.780
and that was very interesting.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.300
You really did see the
market start to take off

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:49.950
after the statement came out

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.080
and sort of start to come
back down a little bit

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.900
after Fed Chair Jerome
Powell started to talk

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.510
and really talk about what he
was thinking about the market.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.090
But the statement with almost no changes

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.340
locked in some of these
market expectations,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.560
and really you saw stock prices go up,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.420
you saw those bond prices come down.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.850
Both of those things
mean people are buying.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.920
When stock prices go up,
it means folks are buying.

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.620
When bond yields go down means,
it bond prices are rising,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.270
which means again, folks are buying.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:16.410
So that was interesting

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.240
and we'll talk more about
that in just a second.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.670
But that minimal change is unusual

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.100
when the Federal Reserve
makes a change in policy.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.470
It typically includes
a lot of explanation.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:26.550
So, what's next?

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.740
Well, what's next is data dependence.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.660
Now, I know you all are
here on "Market Takes",

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.157
you understand the importance of the data

00:04:32.157 --> 00:04:35.190
and you understand the
importance of the CPI, PCE,

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.470
the jobs report, all those things.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:38.490
But for a lot of folks

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.980
who don't really follow
the markets that closely,

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.020
they could see some wild swings

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.640
when we get the next jobs report,

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.920
when we get this PCE
report later this week.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.000
When we get CPI or PPI, those
could move the market a lot

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.520
because we don't really know
what the Fed wants to do.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.400
The Fed isn't sort of hedging
in one direction or the other

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.870
the way they usually do.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:59.910
Usually there'll be
market expectations for,

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.170
okay, they're saying this,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:03.210
but what we really expect is a hike.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:06.750
Coming into this month,
we had a 100% expectation

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.910
for at least a 25 basis point hike

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.950
and even a little bit of an expectation,

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.250
3.5% expectation for
a 50 basis point hike.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.680
So there was no one in the
markets who thought the Fed

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:17.790
wouldn't raise rates today.

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.080
That was priced in.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.910
The only question was what would they say

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.530
and how would that affect the outlook?

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.060
And really what we've heard today

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.030
was Fed Chair Jerome Powell
does not know what comes next.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:30.750
He's gonna be all about inflation
data, all about jobs data,

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:33.300
all about retail sales
data and growth data,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.320
all about housing data,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.926
and thankfully we here at "Market Takes"

00:05:35.926 --> 00:05:37.140
are gonna be here to
break it down for you.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.200
So, please stay tuned and stick with us.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.240
Those of you who have been
watching "Market Takes",

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.200
I know you'll keep tuning in.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.210
Those of you who haven't,
hey, tell a friend.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.190
You wanna want to know
what's gonna move markets

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.620
and that's gonna be what we
talk about from "Market Takes".

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.180
This really could be the first time

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.520
the Fed is truly data dependent.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.170
It's a phrase that they've used a lot.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.000
It's kind of been beaten
into our heads a lot,

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.850
but folks really a lot of times
don't believe in investors

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:00.900
and folks out there in the markets.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.180
They say, "Yeah, yeah, they
say they'll be data dependent,"

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.250
and if this thing really over performs

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.170
and we get a lot more jobs than expected

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.660
or a lot less jobs or a supremely

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.570
or significantly negative
print, then they'll react.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.310
But this really shows us,

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:16.170
and what we heard today from the Chairman,

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:19.740
really sets us up to where
it's all about the data.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.110
Individual reports are gonna move markets

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.210
and are gonna move
markets across the board.

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:25.980
We're talking not just stocks but bonds.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.800
We're talking currencies,
we're talking commodities.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.390
Potentially even the crypto market

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:33.870
could be moved significantly
by some of these big releases.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:36.210
I wanna point out one
thing the Fed Chairman said

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.120
that I don't think really got
a lot of play in the markets

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.130
but I thought this really
caught me off guard.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.440
He talked about if we
see inflation coming down

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:45.870
credibly and sustainably, in his words,

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:46.980
then we can move down

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:49.500
to a neutral level or even
below a neutral level.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.810
What that means is potentially rate cuts,

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.450
as in moving the Fed funds rate back down.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:55.950
And here's why that matters,

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:58.800
is rate cuts are usually aligned

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.560
with the start of a big
takeoff in stock prices,

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:05.850
with the start of a robust
increase in stock prices

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:07.080
and economic activity,

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:08.850
and usually you see the economy takeoff,

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:12.090
and you see even more than that
those stock prices take off

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:14.250
and those other risk
assets really take off.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.907
So Fed Chair Jerome Powell kind of saying,

00:07:15.907 --> 00:07:20.550
"Hey, even before we see
Inflation get down to 2%,"

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:22.970
which has been what they've
been kind of hammering home

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:26.250
and pounding the table on
for the last few months,

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.020
even before we get to 2%,

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.390
we're at about 3% right now on that CPI,

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:31.980
but even before we get to two,

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:35.160
we could start to see some
rate cuts from the Fed.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.840
He said it likely won't be this year

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.210
but you could start to see
rate cuts from the Fed.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:42.120
That really hadn't been
on the table previously

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:44.730
before this meeting, so
that's very interesting.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.250
He said he doesn't see that
until around 2025 or so

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.830
of inflation getting back down to 2%.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.890
So even though inflation
might be stuck around 3%,

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:56.670
around two and a half,
2.7%, so on and so forth,

00:07:56.670 --> 00:08:00.157
in those higher levels, the
Fed has said, "Not acceptable,

00:08:00.157 --> 00:08:01.267
"not good enough.

00:08:01.267 --> 00:08:02.970
"We won't stop there."

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.010
Now Fed Chair Powell is
saying, "Well, you know what?

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:06.607
As long as inflation's moving down

00:08:06.607 --> 00:08:08.857
"sustainably and credibly," in his words,

00:08:08.857 --> 00:08:10.807
"we could start to cut rates

00:08:10.807 --> 00:08:12.510
"maybe as soon as early next year,"

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.570
and so that's a very interesting
takeaway from this meeting.

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:17.430
I thought it was a pretty dovish hike.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.050
Not a supremely dovish
hike, because again,

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.310
it's all about the data,

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.890
gonna be all about CPI, PCI, or sorry,

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.900
CPI, PCE, and PPI.

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.310
That's the Producer Price Index,

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.620
but gonna be all about inflation

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.309
and it's gonna be all about retail sales,

00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:31.290
it's gonna be all about jobs

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.840
and if those numbers are
moving in the direction

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.550
that the Federal Reserve
wants them to move.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:37.230
So, let's take a look at what's going on

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:38.063
in the market right now.

00:08:38.063 --> 00:08:40.380
I'm gonna put up here
on your screen a chart.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:42.840
And just to show you what's
happening in the market,

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:45.000
we had a big deal move today.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.070
I think, I got the Dow
up here on your screen.

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.740
So you can see that the Dow has risen

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:50.910
for 12 straight days

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:52.530
as it closed higher for 12 straight days.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.780
Today is the 13th straight day

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:57.330
that the Dow Jones Industrial
Average closed higher.

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.690
That is the longest streak
we have seen since 1987.

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.630
Those of you who remember 1987
know that that wasn't exactly

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:06.450
a great year for the stock
market, but in the early days

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:09.090
before the big crash that we saw in '87,

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:10.110
that was a big move.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:11.310
And so stocks are moving higher.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.250
This is a move and a
winning streak for the Dow,

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.470
specifically for the Dow, not all stocks,

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:17.970
but for the Dow Jones Industrial Average,

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:19.710
the 30 stocks that make up that,

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.090
those are a lot of industrial names,

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:23.040
those big blue chip companies,

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:25.530
up higher for 13 straight days.

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:27.630
You see here the S&amp;P 500 and the Nasdaq

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:30.270
both ending the day lower,
and what you'll also notice

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:32.640
is the big pickup after
the statement was released

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.860
right on here, and then
sort of coming down

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.690
as Fed Chair Jerome Powell kept talking.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.250
And the market's kinda
recovering a little bit

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:40.470
but not enough to get back positive.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:42.810
When we look at bonds and
the movement in bond prices,

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:44.400
you look at the 10 year yield here,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:45.810
pretty much across the board,

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.820
this is the move you saw
from the bond market.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:49.320
Right around here again
is where we're seeing

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:50.640
that two o'clock timeframe,

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:52.860
and you see bond yields just going down.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:56.040
And unlike for stocks, bond
yields really staying lower

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:57.210
based on what the Chairman said.

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:59.670
You see that even for the two year here

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:02.550
in terms of what the action
was right around two o'clock.

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:06.000
Statement comes out here, and
then you see the move lower

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.230
in terms of bond yields.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:09.090
It means prices are going up.

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:10.530
We see when we look at currencies,

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:11.670
we're seeing the euro going up,

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:13.350
which means the dollar is going down.

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:15.210
A weaker dollar is generally good

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:16.710
for those stock prices as well.

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:18.630
And when we look out at
commodities really quickly,

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.330
we see oil futures,
not a big reaction here

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.860
to the Fed's movement.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:24.120
Right around this two o'clock timeframe,

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:25.830
you don't really see a big reaction

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:26.663
for a lot of commodities.

00:10:26.663 --> 00:10:29.460
But again, this just
illustrating the market reaction

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:32.580
that we saw and that we
got overall from markets.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:34.890
And that was a bullish reaction.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.500
This was priced in, but
I think a lot of folks

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:39.780
didn't expect to see that
statement from the Fed

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:43.080
being as unchanged as it was,
and I think that's really got

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.930
a lot of folks in the markets excited.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:47.640
Let's talk about the press
conference really quickly

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:48.690
from the Fed Chairman.

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:52.260
The Chairman's really,
he said data dependence,

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.000
and I think he meant it this time.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:55.920
There have been some other
times he said data dependence

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:56.753
and folks were like,

00:10:56.753 --> 00:10:58.110
"Yeah, yeah, yeah, sure, data dependence."

00:10:58.110 --> 00:10:59.610
This time I think it's really real.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:01.560
Stocks have been booming
over the last couple months,

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:03.690
and this was kind of the one thing

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:05.040
that could've gotten in the way,

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.530
but now we've got a
number of other hurdles

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:10.620
potentially in the path for
the stock prices to rise.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:11.640
But again, a number of things

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:13.470
that could boost stocks even further.

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:16.830
The Dow has only been up
about six or 7% year to date,

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:18.690
so it hasn't been a huge takeoff

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:20.550
even though the move
has been overwhelmingly

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:21.690
in one direction.

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:24.150
But we haven't seen the Dow
just take off and explode

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:25.350
the way the Nasdaq has.

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.930
The Nasdaq is up over
30% so far this year,

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:30.750
close to 35% just year to date

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:32.820
and we're close to the end of July.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:34.050
We haven't seen that move from the Dow,

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:37.710
but definitely a positive
momentum going into this meeting,

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:40.350
and there'll be positive momentum
I think coming outta this.

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:43.200
Want to talk about what I've
termed soft landing summer.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.600
Let's put a chart up here on
your screen really quickly

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:46.650
just to show you what I mean,

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:48.750
because it really can be encapsulated best

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:49.920
with one piece of data.

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.140
This is the CPI, the Consumer Price Index,

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:53.400
and you see those numbers.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:56.520
Since June 2022, that's that
high bar you see up there

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:59.340
around 9% just coming down consistently.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:01.560
And now we're at 3%, close to,

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:04.740
that's the lowest level we
had seen since early 2021.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:07.710
And really that's exactly
what the Fed wants to see.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:10.110
And this is how the retail
sales data is looking.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:12.150
The jobs report is sort of
looking similar to this,

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:15.000
where the number of jobs
added each month is declining,

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:17.550
but you're not seeing just a tanking.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:20.370
You're not seeing these
numbers end up in the gutter

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.170
or really just start turning negative.

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:25.410
What you're seeing is a soft
landing, a deceleration,

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:28.500
a steady decline towards
more normal levels.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:30.360
And this chart I think really says it all.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:32.820
It illustrates what we've
seen from inflation,

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:35.340
which is inflation
getting up and being high

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:38.340
and then coming back down,
and now being at a level

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:40.200
that's not quite at the Fed's target.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:41.850
I don't think the mission
is accomplished yet.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:43.560
I think Fed Chair Jerome
Powell would agree,

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:45.030
the mission is not accomplished,

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:48.930
but we are moving very strongly
in the right direction.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.690
I think the Fed's message
today was if this continues,

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:54.420
rate cuts could be coming, and
that's definitely something

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.750
for investors who are bulls,
who are on the bullish side

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:00.240
of the stock market betting long equities

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.010
can get excited about.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:04.290
The biggest takeaway from
the press conference I think

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:07.530
is the data dependence and is
just that every single piece

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:09.330
of data is going to be very important.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:12.690
If we get a high CPI
report, if that core CPI

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:14.460
comes in above expectations,

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.080
that could really lead to a selloff

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:17.370
and you could see movement

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:19.620
significantly lower on the stock market.

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.710
We are just in a macroeconomic
environment right now,

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:25.800
where the data has consistently
been positive and solid

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:27.180
and told a good story

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:29.070
about what's happening in the US economy.

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:30.240
And if that continues,

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.640
I think you're gonna see
stock prices continue to rise.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:33.660
You're gonna see those bond yields

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:36.240
continue to come down
as folks keep buying,

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.180
and that's a good thing
on the macroeconomic side.

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:41.580
But again, a report in the other direction

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.430
could really jolt things and
you could see an overreaction,

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:48.240
a knee jerk overreaction, in
a lot of markets to that data.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:50.730
So you're gonna wanna know
what those data points mean

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.553
and what they say. Powell said
during his press conference,

00:13:52.553 --> 00:13:54.630
he said, "The Fed will be data dependent."

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.150
We're gonna be dependent,
quote, "so much on the data",

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:59.040
and we just don't have it yet.

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:00.150
I thought that was a big moment

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:01.800
from the press conference as well.

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:02.820
Markets didn't react much,

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:04.770
but really Powell kinda locking that in.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:08.910
If inflation keeps dropping
towards 2%, not to 2%,

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:10.770
the Fed could start cutting rates.

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:12.000
So, what comes next?

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.930
Well, tomorrow we have got
the second quarter GDP report.

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:17.970
That could, like we said earlier this week

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.370
on the market look ahead,
that could really confirm

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:21.690
this soft landing summer.

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:23.610
Expected to be right around 2% or 1.7%

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:26.520
when we get the number on Thursday.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:28.020
If that comes in, that's again

00:14:28.020 --> 00:14:29.610
right in line with expectations,

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:32.730
solidly positive, but not
too high and not too hot,

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:33.563
not too cold.

00:14:33.563 --> 00:14:34.710
It's just right,

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:37.320
just what the data that we've
seen so far has shown us.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:40.770
We also get the June PCE
inflation report on Friday.

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:42.750
That's in that personal
income and outlays report.

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:45.060
You're gonna wanna look
out for that on Friday.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:47.250
That could move markets
significantly if it's high,

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:48.780
and if it's low, you could see markets

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:50.070
really start to take off.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:51.690
We won't be doing
"Market Takes" on Monday.

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:52.980
I'm taking a little time off,

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:54.900
but we will be back with you next week,

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:56.430
so not this upcoming Monday,

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.110
but the following Monday after that.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:14:59.490
Big jobs report coming out.

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:00.630
You're gonna wanna look into that.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:03.750
So listen, that's gonna do it
for us here on "Market Takes".

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:06.240
I'm Dion Rabouin, reporter
at "The Wall Street Journal".

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:07.920
Thank you all so much for being with us

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:10.470
and we hope you'll come
back and join us next time.

